From: Tiberiu Chibici <chibicitiberiu@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:41:24 +0300
Subject: renderergl2: r_vaoSnormColor, vertex colours as signed normalized
 shorts

renderergl2 sends vertex colours as GL_UNSIGNED_SHORT normalized (R16G16B16A16_UNORM), a vertex
format Mesa nv30 (the PS3's RSX) can't fetch. Every draw from the tess VAO (the world, effects,
the HUD: everything but MD3 models) then went through u_vbuf: the colours converted to floats on
the CPU and uploaded again, and the vertex and index buffers mapped for reading. nouveau answers a
read map of a video memory buffer with a copy of the whole buffer in malloc'ed memory, read back
from VRAM by a GPU copy and a wait; after four flushes in a row with such a read it sets
hint_buf_keep_sysmem_copy for good, and from then on every new VRAM buffer gets a malloc'ed copy
at creation: the MD3 animation VBOs (62 MB for the demo's models) among them. On the PS3
OpenArena opengl2 held 95 MB of malloc'ed memory (opengl1: 33).

r_vaoSnormColor 1 (default, latched) sends the colours as GL_SHORT normalized, v >> 1, which nv30
fetches natively (V16_SNORM, already used for the normals). 0 becomes -1, so a zero alpha stays at
or below zero under both GL rules for signed normalized values and fails an alpha test as before.
The tess colours are converted at upload (8 bytes a vertex, next to the copy u_vbuf made); the VAO
cache (r_vaoCache 1) keeps unsigned shorts. On llvmpipe at GL 2.1, the demo's 30 frames sampled:
17 pixels differ by one level. 0 sends unsigned shorts as before.

